{"clock":1,"nonce":"87793a41f50f66eddf74d67c3942d00f","ops":[{"kind":"create","author":"ee004d31386b76440a74651c79871f156633d4ff","time":1792577109,"origin":{"forge":"github.com/guilbep/git-board","event":"issue:5958447886"},"data":{"body":"Kinds as GitHub issue types (native-fields, merged locally into `bridge-sync` at 0420c69f) have only been tested on a fake GitHub. Run a live test on the EPFL-ENAC scratch repository from #29 before tagging.\n\n- [x] A bug labeled `bug` sets the type Bug, and `polish` stays a label.\n- [ ] A second kind, `feat`, goes as a label; swapping `bug` for `feat` makes the type Feature.\n- [x] A type chosen when the issue is created on GitHub comes in, and its timeline shows an `IssueTypeAddedEvent`.\n- [x] A type changed in GitHub's UI comes in with a normal (not full) sync, which tells whether a type change bumps `updatedAt`.\n- [x] An old `bug` label moves to the type, after `bridge pull \u003cname\u003e --no-resume`, a dry run, then a sync.\n- [x] `kinds = labels` moves types to labels, and switching back to native moves them back.\n- [ ] A fine-grained token can read `issueType` and run `updateIssueIssueType`. Check GitHub's real refusal text when it can't.\n- [ ] Each sync after these writes nothing.\n\nWith `kind-types = bug=Bug,feat=Feature`. Never co2-calculator, never Project 40.","title":"native-fields: live test of GitHub issue types"}},{"kind":"change-labels","author":"ee004d31386b76440a74651c79871f156633d4ff","time":1792670533,"origin":{"forge":"github.com/guilbep/git-board","event":"issue:5958447886"},"data":{"add":["kind:chore","prio:p1","status:backlog","assignee:guilbep"],"remove":null}},{"kind":"comment","author":"ee004d31386b76440a74651c79871f156633d4ff","time":1792611765,"origin":{"forge":"github.com/guilbep/git-board","event":"comment:6324950046"},"data":{"body":"Live test run on 2026-10-21 against **guilbep-sandbox/native-fields-test**, a private repo in a free org that has Task, Bug and Feature types. It used git-bug v0.11.1-sync.9 (0420c69f) with `kind-types = bug=Bug,feat=Feature`. For check 5, a git-bug built from v0.11.1-sync.8 synced the issue first. The issues are left on the repo with titles starting `nf-1021-132105`.\n\n- [x] **1.** A bug labeled `bug` and `polish` gets type Bug on GitHub; `polish` stays a label and the `bug` label is never sent.\n- [ ] **2.** Half passes. Adding `feat` beside `bug` works: type Bug, label `feat`. **Swapping takes two syncs.** After `bug` is removed, the first sync removes type Bug and leaves `feat` as a label (`type=none labels=feat,polish`). The next sync then removes the `feat` label and sets type Feature. One sync should do both.\n- [x] **3.** A type set when the issue is created on GitHub (Feature) comes in as `feat`, and the timeline shows an `IssueTypeAddedEvent`.\n- [x] **4.** A type change on GitHub (Feature to Bug, through `updateIssueIssueType`) bumps `updatedAt` (11:22:16Z to 11:22:36Z), so a normal sync brings it in as `bug`.\n- [x] **5.** A `bug` label recorded by a real sync.8 moves to the type. The `pull --no-resume` imported 0 issues. The dry run names the move (`would send: labels +bug`) and leaves GitHub untouched; the sync sets type Bug and removes the label. git-bug keeps `bug`.\n- [x] **6.** `kinds = labels` moves all three types to labels (`feat`, `bug`, `bug`), and `kinds = native` moves them back. git-bug's labels stay the same throughout.\n- [ ] **7.** Not checked. The fine-grained token in `GH_TOKEN` can't see guilbep-sandbox (`NOT_FOUND`). On EPFL-ENAC the same token is refused: \"guilbep does not have permission to retrieve issueType information.\" (FORBIDDEN on `organization.issueTypes`). To close this check we need a fine-grained token whose resource owner is guilbep-sandbox, with Issues read and write on the repo.\n- [ ] **8.** On GitHub, every sync after a step writes nothing, except the second sync of the check 2 swap. In git-bug:\n  - **The echo of a type move isn't recognized.** After the push moves `bug` to the type, the next sync writes two label ops (`-bug`, `+bug`) for each moved issue: #1 after check 5, and #1 and #3 after switching back to native. The labels end up the same, nothing goes to GitHub, and the sync after that is quiet. The cause is that the push's own Unlabeled + IssueTypeAdded events come back as two separate changes.\n  - A sync after a push also writes SetMetadata commits. sync.8 writes exactly the same ones, so this isn't from native-fields.\n\nTo fix in the fork: the check 2 swap in one sync, and recognizing the echo of a type move. Check 7 needs the token above. The fork's `native-fields.md` still says the live test waits on an EPFL-ENAC repo.\n"}}]}